SEC1_TEXT = r"""
Multi-Stage Intrusion Response and Posture Escalation in IoT Networks: An Empirical Study on CICIoT2023

Anonymous Authors for Double-Blind Review
Under Review — Direct Extension and Revision of Foundational Manuscript [1]

Abstract—Multi-stage intrusion detection systems (NIDS) for IoT networks coordinate supervised classifiers, unsupervised anomaly detectors, and dynamic trust metrics to guide automated response. However, upstream telemetry miscalibration can induce severe operational failure modes. Using the CICIoT2023 benchmark, we investigate the root causes of these failures and establish principled mitigations. First, controlled evaluation reveals that an inverted anomaly signal (AUC = 0.008) stems from training set label mismatch (training on 97.64% attacks); retraining on verified clean traffic reorients anomaly detection (AUC 0.952, Kolmogorov-Smirnov D = 0.866). Second, initializing dynamic trust with neutral priors (T_0 = 100.0) eliminates ground-truth label leakage, elevating trust discrimination from AUC 0.044 to 0.986 across both Regime 1 (balanced) and Regime 2 (attack-heavy) evaluation streams. Third, a six-level posture escalation hierarchy establishes monotonic trust decay from 79.44 at Level L0 to 10.17 at Level L4; Level L5 is populated only under simulated core-hub monitoring (E8) and 0 batches under peripheral edge-gateway streams. Fourth, an exhaustive combinatorial search over all 70 monotonic level-to-action policies under an operational cost model shows that cost-optimal policy P_opt (Allow-Allow-Restrict-Restrict-Block-Block) achieves Rank 1 overall (+1,920,767 combined OCU, 0.00% Block FPR, 0.00% Quarantine FPR), while canonical policy P2-tier delivers +1,909,843 OCU (a 2.43-fold gain over baseline P1) but ranks 9th of 70 due to collateral benign quarantine. A hybrid posture-veto baseline (P_hybrid) achieves the highest combined net benefit (+1,931,108 OCU) by vetoing 880 benign flows in Level L4. All code, datasets, and evaluation artifacts are openly archived.

Index Terms—Internet of Things, Intrusion Response Systems, Anomaly Detection, Isolation Forest, Training Data Purity, Dynamic Trust, Security Posture Escalation, Decision Theory, Cost-Aware Optimization, CICIoT2023.

1. INTRODUCTION

1.1 Motivation and Operational Context
The proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) devices across modern critical infrastructures—ranging from manufacturing and energy distribution grids to municipal transportation and connected healthcare facilities—has expanded the attack surface of networked systems, motivating multi-stage network intrusion detection and response frameworks [1]. Unlike traditional enterprise IT environments with managed workstations and uniform operating systems, IoT deployments are characterized by extreme hardware heterogeneity, resource-constrained microcontrollers, unpatched legacy firmware, and persistent connectivity requirements. These devices generate high-frequency telemetry while presenting common vulnerabilities that malicious actors exploit to construct distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) botnets, execute remote command injections, and conduct lateral reconnaissance [2], [3]. In these asymmetric environments, false-positive alarms introduce severe operational hazards: as formalized by Axelsson's base-rate fallacy in intrusion detection [4], even a tiny false-alarm probability generates an overwhelming volume of spurious alerts when malicious events constitute a small fraction of total network transactions.

Historical security incidents emphasize the operational risk of unmitigated IoT vulnerabilities. The Mirai botnet compromised hundreds of thousands of consumer IoT devices via default credentials, generating high-volume floods that disrupted critical Internet infrastructure [2], [3]. More recently, targeted attacks against operational technology (OT) systems demonstrate that modern intrusions routinely transition from peripheral endpoint scanning to core service disruption. In these interconnected environments, an automated intrusion response system (IRS) faces acute operational trade-offs: isolating devices based on false alarms can interrupt critical industrial workflows, while failing to quarantine compromised nodes risks cascading compromise.

To address these diverse threat vectors, modern network intrusion detection systems (NIDS) have transitioned from standalone signature matchers toward multi-stage analytical pipelines [5], [6]. In the foundational manuscript [1], a multi-stage IoT security architecture was proposed to synthesize complementary analytical perspectives. That pipeline incorporated a supervised gradient-boosted decision tree (XGBoost) [7] for multi-class threat classification, an unsupervised Isolation Forest [8] for novel anomaly detection, an Adaptive Dynamic Trust Model (ADTM) for continuous behavioral tracking across contiguous 100-flow inspection batches, a Topology-Aware Risk Propagation (TARP) engine based on network graph centrality [9], [10], and a policy engine to map these indicators into response actions. Evaluated on the CICIoT2023 benchmark [11], the supervised machine learning tier demonstrated high nominal classification accuracy (98.99% across 34 traffic classes).

Despite the nominal accuracy of the supervised classifier, subsequent auditing revealed three systemic operational anomalies:
1. Dynamic Trust Compression: Inspection batch trust scores calculated by the ADTM collapsed into a compressed numerical range that rarely degraded below 40.0 on a 100-point scale. Consequently, the lower operational trust tiers remained unpopulated even during sustained flooding campaigns.
2. Action Precision Inversion: The automated decision engine exhibited an inverted precision profile. The low-severity Alert action exhibited high attack precision, whereas the higher-severity Quarantine action exhibited lower attack precision due to misclassified benign flows.
3. Policy Inaction: Out of five designed enforcement tiers (Allow, Alert, Restrict, Quarantine, Block), the terminal action—Block—was never executed in baseline configurations, while non-disruptive actions (Allow and Restrict) remained largely inactive.

These operational discrepancies indicate that high supervised classification accuracy in isolation does not guarantee a functional or cost-effective intrusion response pipeline. When upstream anomaly detectors are misaligned and downstream policy mappings are designed heuristically without decision-theoretic grounding, errors compound across stages. This paper conducts a systematic post-mortem investigation of these failure modes, isolates their root causes, and presents a dual-regime evaluation of posture escalation and response optimization.

1.2 Research Questions
This investigation addresses three core questions in automated IoT intrusion response:

Question 1: How can multi-modal telemetry signals be synthesized into batch-level evidence aggregation?
Prior multi-stage intrusion detection systems routinely treat classifiers, anomaly detectors, and graph metrics as disjointed advisory signals [11], [12]. Without a unifying operational abstraction, downstream response logic inevitably devolves into brittle nests of hard-coded boolean conditionals. An effective defense requires an operational security posture model that systematically aggregates multi-source evidence into an ordered, monotonically escalating hierarchy of threat states.

Question 2: What mechanisms cause unsupervised anomaly detectors and behavioral trust models to fail in mixed network streams?
In uncalibrated pipelines, unsupervised Isolation Forests and dynamic trust models can exhibit degraded discrimination between attack traffic and legitimate flows. Investigating whether this degradation arises from mathematical formulation defects, feature representation bottlenecks, or subtle training data provenance corruptions is essential for establishing dependable unsupervised cyber-defense in real-world environments.

Question 3: How should tiered response actions be mapped to minimize operational loss under regime-dependent threat uncertainty?
Automated intrusion response systems must balance threat containment against false-positive disruption [13]. In operational IoT networks, false-positive containment actions (such as disconnecting an industrial actuator or isolating a hospital telemetry monitor) incur operational disruption penalties. Conversely, under-responding to high-volume attacks risks system unavailability. A robust response architecture must formally model these asymmetric operational costs, evaluating action mappings across differing network regimes and comparing batch posture policies against pure per-flow classifier baselines and hybrid veto mechanisms.

1.3 Summary of Contributions
This paper investigates these questions through three empirical contributions, evaluated on test partitions of the CICIoT2023 dataset [11]:

Contribution 1 — Formulation of a Six-Level Posture Escalation Model:
We formulate a security posture architecture that aggregates multi-stage telemetry into an integer cumulative evidence score $S \in [0, 4]$, synthesizing anomaly extremity, trust deficit, classification confidence, and graph topological risk (TARP). The score maps monitoring batches of $W=100$ flows into six formal escalation levels: $L_0$ (Normal Monitoring), $L_1$ (Heightened Surveillance), $L_2$ (Contextual Evaluation), $L_3$ (Active Containment), $L_4$ (Severe Containment - Peripheral / Off-Hub), and $L_5$ (Critical Topological Hub Containment). In a 120-node Barabási-Albert scale-free topology ($m=2, \alpha=0.85$, PageRank $\mu_R = 0.4189, \sigma_R = 0.0721$ [9], [10]), only two core hub nodes exceed the critical threshold $R \ge 0.75$ (Node 0 with $R=0.9000$ and Node 4 with $R=0.8089$, individual node criticality $Z = 4.59\sigma, p = 2.2 \times 10^{-6}$ on the empirical graph; $Z=4.125\sigma$ under theoretical target $\mu=0.42, \sigma=0.08$; $41.25\sigma$ under CLT on 100-node batches). Level $L_5$ is populated in Experiment E8 (simulated core-hub monitoring, 100,000 flows, 100% attack); under peripheral edge-gateway streams Level $L_5$ remains empty. When core hubs process clean benign traffic, 100% of batches populate Level $L_1$ (triggering Alert under canonical policy $P_{2\text{-tier}}$ [100.0% Alert FPR, 0.00% Block and Quarantine FPR], or Allow under recommended policy $P_{\text{opt}}$ [0.00% Alert, Quarantine, and Block FPR]). On the multi-state benchmark stream, batch assignment across active operational levels ($L_0$ to $L_4$) exhibits non-decreasing attack density ($0.00\% \le 0.00\% \le 46.40\% \le 51.71\% \le 100.00\%$) and monotonic trust decay ($79.44 \to 10.17$), reflecting the three synthetic stream episodes (pure benign, 50% mixed, pure attack).

Contribution 2 — Diagnosis of Isolation Forest Contamination and Neutral Trust Recalibration:
We isolate an implementation defect in the foundational manuscript [1]: a categorical string mismatch ('BENIGN' versus 'BenignTraffic') caused the unsupervised Isolation Forest [8] to train on 97.64% attack data, learning an inverted orientation where benign traffic appeared as anomalous outliers (raw AUC 0.008, or 0.992 when inverted; KS $D = 0.950$). Controlled retraining on verified clean benign traffic reorients the model semantically to nominal behavior across $M=100$ and $M=200$ trees (AUC 0.952, Kolmogorov-Smirnov $D = 0.866, p < 10^{-15}$). Empirical dose-response analysis reveals that performance degradation initiates at 5–10% contamination, directional reversal occurs at 20% (raw AUC 0.246, inverted AUC 0.754), and near-complete inversion occurs at 97.6% (AUC 0.008). In benchmark comparisons, specialized tabular detectors (ECOD AUC 0.9770, Mahalanobis AUC 0.9630) outperform Isolation Forest, confirming that while IF repair reorients the foundational model, tabular-specific detectors offer superior outlier bounds. Rebuilding the dynamic trust model with neutral initial priors ($T_0 = 100.0$) and eliminating ground-truth attack family leakage elevates raw trust discrimination from AUC 0.044 to 0.986 on attack-heavy traffic (and 0.9990 on balanced streams), establishing genuine runtime degradation driven by multi-modal operational signals rather than label-derived priors.

Contribution 3 — Combinatorial Policy Optimization, Baseline Benchmarking, and Regime-Dependent Trade-offs:
We execute an exhaustive combinatorial search across all 70 admissible monotonic level-to-action mappings under a formalized cost matrix expressed in Operational Cost Units (OCU). The search establishes that policy $P_{\text{opt}}$ (`Allow-Allow-Restrict-Restrict-Block-Block`) achieves Rank 1 overall (+1,920,767 combined OCU, with 0.00% Block FPR and 0.00% Quarantine FPR on balanced traffic). We recommend $P_{\text{opt}}$ as the primary default zero-Quarantine response policy. Canonical policy $P_{2\text{-tier}}$ (`Allow-Alert-Restrict-Quarantine-Block-Block`) ranks 9th of 70 (+1,909,843 combined OCU); while $P_{2\text{-tier}}$ is the unique policy preserving 1-to-1 strict progression across all five enforcement actions, it is Pareto-dominated by 8 policies that avoid collateral benign quarantine disruption on multi-state traffic. Policy $P_{2\text{-tier}}$ achieves non-decreasing precision across all five actions ($0.00\% \le 0.00\% \le 46.40\% \le 51.71\% \le 100.00\%$) and delivers a 2.43-fold combined net benefit advantage over conservative baseline $P_1$ (mean of 5 seeds: $2.427 \pm 0.001$, $+1,909,278 \pm 1,127$ vs $+785,992 \pm 1,023$ OCU; 2.27x on multi-state, 2.48x on attack-heavy), conditional on false-blocking penalty factor $\lambda \le 30.8$ (Block penalty $\le 1,540$ OCU). On held-out test data ($D_{\text{test}}$, seed 99), all 70 policy rankings replicate perfectly, with $P_{\text{opt}}$ maintaining Rank 1 (+1,920,663 OCU) and $P_{2\text{-tier}}$ maintaining Rank 9 (+1,907,922 OCU). Evaluating previously missing baselines, we demonstrate that a Pure Per-Flow XGBoost policy ($P_{\text{XGB}}$) achieves +2,183,725 OCU combined (+2,887,550 attack-heavy, +1,479,900 balanced) but inflicts a 9.46% Block FPR on benign flows (blocking 2,827 legitimate flows on balanced traffic), severely violating operational availability. In contrast, an architecturally novel Hybrid Posture + Benign Veto policy ($P_{\text{hybrid}}$) evaluates batch posture escalation while inspecting flows inside Level $L_4$ with a per-flow benign veto ($c_t \ge 0.90$), delivering +1,931,108 OCU combined (dominating all 70 static policies in net benefit, while incurring 30.10% benign Quarantine FPR compared to 0.00% under $P_{\text{opt}}$) and slashing flood Block FPR from 100.0% down to 34.03%–38.89% by vetoing 880 benign flows with only 17 false negatives. On an independent held-out balanced test partition (Experiment E21, seed 99), $P_{2\text{-tier}}$'s Block FPR is 0.23% (Wilson 95% CI: [0.18%, 0.29%], Quarantine FPR 30.72%, +959,843 OCU balanced net benefit, +1,907,922 OCU combined with benchmark / +1,911,721.5 OCU with test). Crucially, on an unstratified stream shuffled without batch episode homogeneity, $P_{2\text{-tier}}$ incurs a 74.60%–75.18% Quarantine FPR, demonstrating that $P_{2\text{-tier}}$ is unsuitable for unsegmented arrival streams and providing strong operational justification for $P_{\text{opt}}$ (0.00% Quarantine FPR) and $P_{\text{hybrid}}$.
"""
